    "result_text": "All 26 tests pass. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` with a per-city sequence.\n\n**`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 the derivation logic:\n- `CITY3` = upper-cased first three letters of `owner.getCity()`\n- `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of the last name (unchanged)\n- `NNNN` = per-city sequence: one more than the count of existing owners whose city matches (was previously a global count of all owners)\n\n```java\nString city3 = city.substring(0, Math.min(3, city.length())).toUpperCase();\nString last3 = lastName.substring(0, Math.min(3, lastName.length())).toUpperCase();\nint sequence = (int) ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(existing -> city.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getCity()))\n        .count() + 1;\nowner.setCustomerCode(String.format(\"%s-%s-%04d\", city3, last3, sequence));\n```\n\n**`AssignMembershipNumber.java`** \u2014 the membership number is `customerCode + \"-M\" + YY`, so it's rebuilt on the new format automatically; I only updated its javadoc example (`SYD-SMI-0007-M26`).\n\nEverything derived from the customerCode flows from these two steps, so the membership number now sits on the city-prefixed code without further changes. Full suite: 26/26 green.",
